WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.620
The lining of a mouse's small intestine is replaced every few days.

2
00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:09.980
Yet the animal keeps it for life.

3
00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:12.300
Stem cells make that possible.

4
00:00:13.220 --> 00:00:17.140
They sit at the bottom of pockets called crypts, and they do two jobs at once.

5
00:00:17.960 --> 00:00:20.480
They keep making every specialized cell of the lining.

6
00:00:21.280 --> 00:00:22.800
And they keep up their own numbers.

7
00:00:23.700 --> 00:00:27.280
Those two jobs, tested over time, are what make a cell a stem cell.

8
00:00:28.250 --> 00:00:30.170
How would you prove which cells do it?

9
00:00:30.910 --> 00:00:33.110
Expressing a gene tells you nothing about fate.

10
00:00:33.850 --> 00:00:35.590
So scientists trace cells instead.

11
00:00:36.410 --> 00:00:41.850
A pulse of the drug tamoxifen puts a permanent mark in Lgr5 cells at the crypt base.

12
00:00:42.750 --> 00:00:44.330
Every daughter inherits that mark.

13
00:00:45.130 --> 00:00:49.830
Over 60 days, the label filled ribbons of epithelium holding every kind of

14
00:00:49.830 --> 00:00:53.590
epithelial cell. That shows multipotency within the gut.

15
00:00:54.370 --> 00:00:55.930
It does not show pluripotency.

16
00:00:56.950 --> 00:00:58.830
How does the pool stay the same size?

17
00:00:59.750 --> 00:01:03.910
These stem cells divide about once a day, and most divisions are symmetric.

18
00:01:04.890 --> 00:01:06.290
Daughters stay or leave by chance.

19
00:01:07.210 --> 00:01:09.730
Some clones take over a crypt, while others vanish.

20
00:01:10.590 --> 00:01:13.210
The population keeps the balance, not each division.

21
00:01:14.283 --> 00:01:16.383
What tells a daughter to stay or to leave?

22
00:01:17.283 --> 00:01:18.603
Its neighbours, called the niche.

23
00:01:19.443 --> 00:01:22.523
At the crypt base, Wnt signals keep cells as stem cells.

24
00:01:23.503 --> 00:01:26.083
Higher up, BMP signals push them to specialize.

25
00:01:27.083 --> 00:01:29.343
Paneth cells help stem cells grow in a dish.

26
00:01:30.083 --> 00:01:34.863
But in the living gut, stem cells survived without them, and Wnt from cells beneath

27
00:01:34.863 --> 00:01:35.903
the lining was needed.

28
00:01:36.743 --> 00:01:38.263
The niche has more than one source.

29
00:01:39.267 --> 00:01:41.627
Fates are less fixed than that picture suggests.

30
00:01:42.567 --> 00:01:46.447
When the Lgr5 stem cells were killed, the crypt still recovered.

31
00:01:47.447 --> 00:01:52.247
Progenitor cells, on their way to becoming absorptive or secretory cells, went back

32
00:01:52.247 --> 00:01:53.527
and rebuilt the stem pool.

33
00:01:54.307 --> 00:01:55.767
That is an injury result.

34
00:01:56.587 --> 00:01:59.867
It does not mean mature cells act as stem cells every day.

35
00:02:00.850 --> 00:02:03.090
How far back can a specialized cell go?

36
00:02:03.890 --> 00:02:09.990
In 1962, John Gurdon put a nucleus from a tadpole's gut cell into a frog egg without

37
00:02:09.990 --> 00:02:14.010
its own nucleus. A small fraction became normal tadpoles.

38
00:02:14.890 --> 00:02:20.430
In 2006, Shinya Yamanaka's lab used four transcription factors to turn mouse

39
00:02:20.430 --> 00:02:22.510
fibroblasts into pluripotent cells.

40
00:02:23.510 --> 00:02:24.890
Specialized cells can be reset.

41
00:02:25.670 --> 00:02:30.790
Pluripotent is not totipotent, and passing these tests is not the same as being safe

42
00:02:30.790 --> 00:02:31.390
in patients.
